WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.584
Implementation should make validation consistent and maintainable.

00:00:03.584 --> 00:00:11.385
Centralized validation, schema validation, framework validators, typed request models, and reusable business rule checks can all help.

00:00:11.385 --> 00:00:17.078
The goal is not to hide every rule in one giant function.

00:00:17.078 --> 00:00:26.987
The goal is to make validation clear, testable, and close to the boundary where data enters the trusted part of the application.

00:00:26.987 --> 00:00:29.307
Server-side validation is mandatory.

00:00:29.307 --> 00:00:37.108
Client-side validation improves usability by catching mistakes early, but clients can be modified, bypassed, or replaced entirely.

00:00:37.108 --> 00:00:42.379
Attackers and automated tools can send requests directly to the server.

00:00:42.379 --> 00:00:50.601
The server must enforce the real rules, even if the browser or mobile application already performed friendly checks.

00:00:50.601 --> 00:00:55.872
Error messages should help legitimate users without revealing unnecessary internal details.

00:00:55.872 --> 00:01:03.673
A message can explain that a field is required, out of range, or in the wrong format.

00:01:03.673 --> 00:01:10.631
It should not expose stack traces, database structure, secret values, parser internals, or security-sensitive logic.

00:01:10.631 --> 00:01:16.745
Invalid input should also be logged carefully, avoiding raw sensitive values where possible.

00:01:16.745 --> 00:01:20.751
Testing validation requires both positive and negative cases.

00:01:20.751 --> 00:01:26.022
Unit tests should confirm valid examples pass and invalid examples fail.

00:01:26.022 --> 00:01:35.088
Negative tests should cover missing fields, wrong types, boundary values, unexpected encodings, excessive length, disallowed values, and invalid field relationships.

00:01:35.088 --> 00:01:40.781
Fuzzing at a high level can help discover parser and boundary issues.

00:01:40.781 --> 00:01:46.052
Good validation is engineered, tested, and revisited as the application evolves.
